{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1001", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "Hark! A fair and wondrous journey to Japan doth thou embark upon. Lo! Here is thy itinerary writ in the tongue of the immortal Bard.\n\n**Day One: The Rising Sun and the Ancient Temples**\n\nFirst to Tokyo’s fair streets do thou repair. In the morn shalt thou visit the sacred Meiji Shrine where whispers of the gods do linger. Then to the bustling lanes of Harajuku where youth and fashion dance like jesters in a play. As the sun doth set feast upon sushi at Jiro’s famed counter if fortune doth smile upon thee. Else sup at a humble izakaya where the sake flows like a merry river.\n\n**Day Two: Castles and Shadows**\n\nUpon the morrow journey to the ancient city of Kamakura. There standeth the Great Buddha gazing upon thee with serene mien. Then to the hallowed grounds of Hasedera where cherry blossoms in season or golden leaves in autumn do paint the earth in hues most fair. In the evening board the train to Kyoto where thou shalt rest thy weary bones.\n\n**Day Three: The Imperial City’s Splendor**\n\nIn Kyoto arise ere the lark doth sing. First to the Fushimi Inari Shrine where torii gates do march like soldiers up the mountain. Then to the Kinkaku-ji the Golden Pavilion gleaming like a jewel set in emerald. As the day wanes partake in a tea ceremony most refined. When night falls witness the geisha of Gion glide like silent spirits through the lantern-lit streets.\n\n**Day Four: The Philosopher’s Path and the Bamboo Grove**\n\nAt dawn stroll the Philosopher’s Path where the willows do weep and the waters run clear. Then to the Arashiyama Bamboo Grove where shafts of light do pierce the verdant cathedral. In the afternoon visit the Kiyomizu-dera where the city’s soul doth spread before thee. As the sun doth dip below the hills take the train to Nara where the deer do bow and the Todai-ji Temple standeth in ancient glory.\n\n**Day Five: The Deer Park and the Celestial Roof**\n\nIn Nara wander with the deer who are gentle and wise. Then to the Todai-ji where the Great Buddha doth sit in quiet majesty. In the afternoon return to Kyoto to don thy kimono if thou hast chosen to do so. At night the Gion district doth come alive with song and dance most mysterious.\n\n**Day Six: The Floating World’s Last Act**\n\nUpon the morrow fare thee to Osaka the merchant’s paradise. Scale the Osaka Castle where samurai once did tread. Then to Dotonbori where the lights do blaze like stars fallen to earth. Sup upon takoyaki and okonomiyaki as the lanterns do sway. When the hour grows late return to Kyoto to prepare for thy departure.\n\n**Day Seven: The Swan Song**\n\nBid farewell to the land of the rising sun. If time doth permit visit the serene Kiyomizu-dera once more to bid thy prayers. Else wander the Nishiki Market to taste the flavors of Japan’s bounty. As the sun doth set make haste to the airport where thy journey’s end doth loom.\n\nFare thee well noble traveler. May thy path be blessed and thy memories of Japan as timeless as the works of Shakespeare himself.", "logprobs": [{"token": "H", "logprob": -1.0742392539978027}, {"token": "ark", "logprob": -0.00033396866638213396}, {"token": "!", "logprob": -0.229268416762352}, {"token": " A", "logprob": -1.3728437423706055}, {"token": " fair", "logprob": -1.5063042640686035}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.767149806022644}, {"token": " w", "logprob": -0.11717281490564346}, {"token": "ond", "logprob": -5.876845170860179e-05}, {"token": "rous", "logprob": -1.156323378381785e-05}, {"token": " journey", "logprob": -0.3606038987636566}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.4880356788635254}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -2.117359161376953}, {"token": " d", "logprob": -0.7625219225883484}, {"token": "oth", "logprob": -4.887569048150908e-06}, {"token": " thou", "logprob": -1.7280933856964111}, {"token": " embark", "logprob": -1.06588876247406}, {"token": " upon", "logprob": -0.024